WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.220
- [Instructor] There are
many videos on Khan Academy

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.030
where we talk about factoring polynomials.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.330
What we're going to do in this video

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.650
is do a few more examples

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.920
of factoring higher degree polynomials.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.770
So let's start with a
little bit of a warmup.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:18.120
Let's say that we wanted
to factor six x squared

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:23.120
plus nine x times x squared
minus four x plus four.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.900
Pause this video and see
if you can factor this

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.433
into the product of even more expressions.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.930
All right, now let's do this together

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.910
and the way that this might
be a little bit different

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:36.850
than what you've seen before

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.290
is this is already partially factored.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.820
This polynomial, this
higher degree polynomial,

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.830
is already expressed as the product

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.260
of two quadratic expressions

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.530
but as you might be able to tell,

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.610
we can factor this further.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.350
For example, six x squared plus nine x,

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:58.350
both six x squared and nine
x are divisible by three x.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.470
So let's factor out a three x here.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.180
So this is the same
thing as three x times,

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.110
three x times what is six x squared?

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:11.110
Well, three times two is six
and x times x is x squared

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.160
and then three x times what is nine x?

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.950
Well, three x times three
is nine x and you can verify

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.140
that if we were to
distribute this three x,

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.920
you would get six x squared plus nine x

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.130
and then what about this second
expression right over here?

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.020
Can we factor this?

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.520
Well, you might recognize
this as a perfect square.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.353
Some of you might have said,

00:01:33.353 --> 00:01:34.760
hey, I need to come up with two numbers

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.670
whose product is four and
whose sum is negative four

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.410
and you might say, hey, that's
negative two and negative two

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.460
and so this would be x minus two.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.040
We could write x minus two squared

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.890
or we could write it as x
minus two times x minus two.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.810
If what I just did is unfamiliar,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.640
I encourage you to go
back and watch videos

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.670
on factoring perfect square
quadratics and things like that

00:01:58.670 --> 00:01:59.503
but there you have it.

00:01:59.503 --> 00:02:03.410
I think we have factored
this as far as we can go.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.240
So now let's do a slightly trickier

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.590
higher degree polynomial.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.910
So let's say we wanted
to factor x to the third

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:16.890
minus four x squared plus six x minus 24

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.050
and just like always, pause this video

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.480
and see if you can have a go at it

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.310
and I'll give you a little bit of a hint.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.350
You can factor in this case by grouping

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.350
and in some ways it's a little bit easier

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.710
than what we've done in the past.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:33.470
Historically, when we've
learned factoring by grouping,

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.630
we've looked at a quadratic

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.510
and then we looked at the middle term,

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.910
the x term of the quadratic
and we broke it up

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.420
so that we had four terms.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:43.430
Here, we already have four terms.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.080
See if you can have a go at that.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.410
All right, now let's do it together.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:51.910
So you can't always factor
a third degree polynomial

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:53.610
by grouping but sometimes you can

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.230
so it's good to look for it.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.430
So when we see it written like this,

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.150
we say okay, x to the
third minus four x squared,

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.450
is there a common factor here?

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.620
Well, yeah, both x to the third
and negative four x squared

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.160
are divisible by x squared.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.250
So what happens if we
factor out an x squared?

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.520
So that's x squared times x minus four

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.100
and what about these second two terms?

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.150
Is there a common factor
between six x and negative 24?

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.070
Yeah, they're both divisible by six.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.370
So let's factor out a six here.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:29.370
So plus six times x minus four

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:33.450
and now you are probably
seeing the homestretch

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.740
where you have something
times x minus four

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:39.140
and then something else times
x minus four and so you can,

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:42.170
sometimes I like to say
undistribute the x minus four

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.130
or factor out the x minus four

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:49.130
and so this is going to be x
minus four times x squared,

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.440
x squared plus six and we are done.

